---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../lib/site";
import { localizePath , pick } from "../../i18n/utils";

const lang = (Astro.currentLocale ?? "en") as "en" | "zh";

const slug = "cloudflare-tunnel-ssh-setup";

const C = pick({
  en: {
    heading: "Connect over Cloudflare Tunnel SSH",
    intro:
      "SSH to a server behind Cloudflare Tunnel from VVTerm by its public hostname — with no inbound ports open on the host.",
    meta: "Guide",
    title: "Connect over Cloudflare Tunnel SSH — VVTerm",
    description:
      "Configure cloudflared for SSH, publish a public hostname, and connect from VVTerm to a server behind Cloudflare Tunnel with no inbound ports open.",
    schemaDescription:
      "Step-by-step guide to SSH over Cloudflare Tunnel with VVTerm: configure cloudflared for SSH, publish a public hostname, and connect to it with no inbound ports open.",
    bodyHtml: `
  <p>
    Cloudflare Tunnel lets a server expose a service through Cloudflare's network without opening any
    inbound ports. VVTerm can connect to an SSH service published this way using its <strong>public hostname</strong>, so a host behind NAT or a strict firewall stays reachable.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    A lightweight <code>cloudflared</code> daemon on the server makes an <strong>outbound</strong> connection
    to Cloudflare and registers a route. Cloudflare then maps a public hostname to your local SSH service.
    Because the tunnel is established from the inside out, the host never needs an open inbound port — there
    is nothing to expose or forward.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A <strong>Cloudflare account</strong> with a domain managed in Cloudflare.</li>
    <li><code>cloudflared</code> installed on the server, and a named tunnel created and authenticated.</li>
    <li>The tunnel <strong>configured for SSH</strong>, routing a public hostname to the local SSH service at <code>localhost:22</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    In a typical <code>cloudflared</code> config, an ingress rule maps the hostname to SSH, for example:
  </p>
  <pre><code>ingress:
  - hostname: ssh.example.com
    service: ssh://localhost:22
  - service: http_status:404</code></pre>
  <p>
    Add the public hostname as a DNS route for the tunnel, then run the tunnel so the route goes live. If you
    use Cloudflare Access in front of the hostname, make sure your policy permits the user that will be
    connecting.
  </p>

  <h2>Connect from VVTerm</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In VVTerm, tap <strong>Add Server</strong> (or edit an existing server).</li>
    <li>Set the <strong>host</strong> to your tunnel's <strong>public hostname</strong>, for example <code>ssh.example.com</code>.</li>
    <li>Enter the <strong>username</strong> you log in with on the server, and keep your usual SSH authentication — password or key.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Cloudflare Tunnel SSH</strong> as the connection method.</li>
    <li>Save and tap the server to connect.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    VVTerm reaches the hostname through Cloudflare and lands you at the server's shell, just like a direct
    SSH session.
  </p>

  <h2>No inbound ports open</h2>
  <p>
    The advantage of this setup is what you <strong>don't</strong> have to do: no port 22 exposed to the
    internet, no firewall rule for inbound SSH, and no router configuration. The only network connection the
    host initiates is outbound to Cloudflare, which keeps the attack surface small.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the hostname won't connect, confirm the tunnel is running and that its ingress rule points at <code>ssh://localhost:22</code>.</li>
    <li>Check that the public hostname has a DNS route attached to the tunnel in the Cloudflare dashboard.</li>
    <li>If access is blocked, review any Cloudflare Access policy covering the hostname.</li>
  </ul>
`,
  },
  zh: {
    heading: "通过 Cloudflare Tunnel SSH 连接",
    intro:
      "在 VVTerm 中通过公开主机名 SSH 连接 Cloudflare Tunnel 后的服务器 —— 主机上无需开放任何入站端口。",
    meta: "指南",
    title: "通过 Cloudflare Tunnel SSH 连接 — VVTerm",
    description:
      "为 SSH 配置 cloudflared、发布公开主机名，并从 VVTerm 连接 Cloudflare Tunnel 后的服务器，无需开放任何入站端口。",
    schemaDescription:
      "在 VVTerm 中通过 Cloudflare Tunnel 进行 SSH 的分步指南：为 SSH 配置 cloudflared、发布公开主机名，并连接它，无需开放任何入站端口。",
    bodyHtml: `
  <p>
    Cloudflare Tunnel 让服务器无需开放任何入站端口即可通过 Cloudflare 网络暴露服务。VVTerm 可以使用<strong>公开主机名</strong>连接以这种方式发布的 SSH 服务，因此位于 NAT 或严格防火墙之后的主机仍可访问。
  </p>

  <h2>工作原理</h2>
  <p>
    服务器上一个轻量的 <code>cloudflared</code> 守护进程会向 Cloudflare 发起<strong>出站</strong>连接并注册一条路由。Cloudflare 随后将一个公开主机名映射到你本地的 SSH 服务。由于隧道是由内向外建立的，主机始终无需开放入站端口 —— 没有任何需要暴露或转发的东西。
  </p>

  <h2>前置条件</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>一个<strong>Cloudflare 账户</strong>，并在 Cloudflare 中管理一个域名。</li>
    <li>服务器上已安装 <code>cloudflared</code>，并创建并认证了一个命名隧道。</li>
    <li>隧道<strong>已为 SSH 配置</strong>，将一个公开主机名路由到本地的 SSH 服务 <code>localhost:22</code>。</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    在典型的 <code>cloudflared</code> 配置中，一条 ingress 规则会把主机名映射到 SSH，例如：
  </p>
  <pre><code>ingress:
  - hostname: ssh.example.com
    service: ssh://localhost:22
  - service: http_status:404</code></pre>
  <p>
    把公开主机名添加为该隧道的 DNS 路由，然后运行隧道使路由生效。如果你在主机名前使用 Cloudflare Access，请确保你的策略允许将要连接的用户。
  </p>

  <h2>从 VVTerm 连接</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>在 VVTerm 中点按 <strong>Add Server</strong>（或编辑现有服务器）。</li>
    <li>将<strong>主机</strong>设为隧道的<strong>公开主机名</strong>，例如 <code>ssh.example.com</code>。</li>
    <li>输入你在服务器上登录用的<strong>用户名</strong>，并保留你惯用的 SSH 认证 —— 密码或密钥。</li>
    <li>选择 <strong>Cloudflare Tunnel SSH</strong> 作为连接方式。</li>
    <li>保存并点按服务器进行连接。</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    VVTerm 会通过 Cloudflare 访问该主机名，并把你带到服务器的 shell，就像一次直接的 SSH 会话一样。
  </p>

  <h2>无需开放入站端口</h2>
  <p>
    这种设置的优势在于你<strong>不必</strong>做的事：无需向互联网暴露 22 端口、无需为入站 SSH 设置防火墙规则、无需配置路由器。主机发起的唯一网络连接是向 Cloudflare 的出站连接，这使攻击面保持很小。
  </p>

  <h2>故障排查</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>如果主机名无法连接，请确认隧道正在运行，且其 ingress 规则指向 <code>ssh://localhost:22</code>。</li>
    <li>在 Cloudflare 控制台中检查该公开主机名是否已为隧道附加了 DNS 路由。</li>
    <li>如果访问被阻止，请检查覆盖该主机名的任何 Cloudflare Access 策略。</li>
  </ul>
`,
  },
}, lang);

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  name: C.heading,
  description: C.schemaDescription,
  url: `${SITE.siteUrl}${localizePath(`/guides/${slug}`, lang)}`,
};
---

<DocsLayout
  kind="guides"
  activeSlug={slug}
  heading={C.heading}
  intro={C.intro}
  meta={C.meta}
  title={C.title}
  description={C.description}
  canonicalPath={`/guides/${slug}`}
  structuredData={[articleSchema]}
>
  <Fragment set:html={C.bodyHtml} />
</DocsLayout>
